# @PROJECT@

@NAME@ on [**n64lle**](https://github.com/RetroPortingToolKit/n64lle) — the
ground-up, LLE-faithful N64 static recompiler.

This is a *per-game sibling repo* in the sense of n64lle `docs/05-PROPOSAL.md`
§10: the framework lives in its own monorepo and is consumed here as a
submodule; this repo holds only what is specific to @NAME@ — the `game.toml`
contract, the recompilation wiring, the per-title gates, and the ROM-derived
generated code. It carries **no host source**: the executable comes from one
call to the framework's `n64lle_add_runtime_target()`, so a fix to the launcher,
input, audio or the run loop arrives on a submodule bump.

**No ROM bytes and no ROM-derived artifacts are committed here.** You bring your
own dump; everything derived from it is produced on your machine.

Scaffolded by `n64lle tools/new_project/setup_project.sh` on @DATE@.

---

## What is established, and what is not

This section is the project's honesty ledger. It starts almost empty on
purpose — a freshly scaffolded repo has *measured nothing*, and saying so is
more useful than a checklist of aspirations. Fill each row in as you establish
it, and keep `docs/STATUS.md` as the place the numbers behind them live.

| | |
|---|---|
| ROM identity read | yes — see `game.toml`, all `[MEASURED]` |
| Recompiles | not yet run |
| Compiled tier equals the interpreter | not yet run — the cosim gates decide |
| Renders | not yet observed — `@SLUG@-frame-probe` is the tool |
| Runs to completion | unknown |
| Saves | no — n64lle models no save device for any cart yet |
| Playable | unknown — that verdict needs a real controller and a human |

Nothing here is graded against **hardware**. The Ares oracle differentials that
carry n64lle's byte-exactness claim do not build off Windows, so "correct" in
this repo means "correct against the interpreter" until that changes. Do not
upgrade that wording.

## Build

Requires CMake ≥ 3.20, Ninja, a C17/C++20 compiler, Python 3, and SDL3 ≥ 3.2
for the host.

```sh
git clone --recurse-submodules <this repo>
cd @PROJECT@

# 1. Stage your own @NAME@ dump. See roms/README.md for the identity check.
cp "/path/to/@ROM_BASENAME@" @ROM_FILE@

# 2. Build the framework out of tree (one-time).
tools/build_framework.sh Release

# 3. Build this repo: harvest -> emit -> compile -> host -> gates.
cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build -j"$(nproc)"
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

# 4. Play it.
./build/@EXE@                     # launcher
./build/@EXE@ --no-launcher       # straight to the game
```

Without SDL3 the configure step warns, declines to build the host, and builds
the gates and the probe exactly as before.

## What the build produces

| Target | What it is |
|---|---|
| `@SLUG@-generate` | runs the harvester and `n64emit`; writes `generated/@SLUG@_generated.c` |
| `@SLUG@-cosim` | lockstep co-simulation of the compiled tier against the interpreter floor |
| `@SLUG@-bench` | the paired, in-process tier A/B — the only place a timing claim may come from |
| `@SLUG@-frame-probe` | free-runs the ROM and dumps the VI scanout as a PPM, plus the ring state behind it |
| `@EXE@` | the host: recomp-ui launcher, then the game in a window, with input and audio |

```sh
N64_RSP_EXEC=1 ./build/@SLUG@-frame-probe @ROM_FILE@ captures/f300.ppm 300 2000
magick captures/f300.ppm captures/f300.png
```

`N64_RSP_EXEC=1` is not optional for the probe. With RSP execution off no
graphics task ever runs and every frame is blank — the flag, not the game. (The
host turns it on itself, from `game.toml`.)

## Gates

```
@SLUG@_cosim_gate1          compiled tier ≡ interpreter, exact cycle boundaries
@SLUG@_cosim_gate1_rsp      the same with RSP execution live
@SLUG@_cosim_gate3_inject   injected-fault detection (the gate must catch it)
@SLUG@_cosim_gate4_audit    hash-vs-byte audit
@SLUG@_bench_smoke          pipeline-rot guard
@SLUG@_contract             this repo's own game.toml still resolves
@SLUG@_host_smoke           the host boots, presents 30 fields, screenshots, exits
```

Everything but the last two reuses n64lle's own ROM-agnostic drivers against
this title's generated dispatch table, so a framework fix reaches this repo on a
submodule bump rather than through a forked copy.

## First things to do

1. **Run the pipeline and read the dispatch-miss report.** Every host run ends
   with `DISPATCH_STATS` and, if there were misses, a line naming them. Project
   law puts triaging those ahead of all other debugging — no performance number
   is trustworthy until they are resolved, and the triage output is what fills
   in `game.toml [audit].regions`.
2. **Screenshot before believing anything about the picture.** Counters and ring
   totals are instrument state, not pixels.
3. **Widen the harvest window if early execution is not covered.**
   `@SLUG_UPPER@_HARVEST_FRAMES` in CMakeLists.txt, mirrored in `game.toml`.
4. **Write down what you measure in `docs/STATUS.md`,** with the date and the
   machine. A number without those is not evidence.

## Submodules

| Path | Upstream | State |
|---|---|---|
| `n64lle/` | `RetroPortingToolKit/n64lle` | the framework: recompiler, runtime, host, gates |
| `recomp-ui/` | `mstan/recomp-ui` | the shared launcher |

